WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.500
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 13.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.647
Elements are input alternating A zero, B zero, A one, B one, and so on, until a value

3
00:00:11.647 --> 00:00:16.993
below minus one times ten to the six is entered, or n reaches one thousand.

4
00:00:16.993 --> 00:00:19.231
Which loop does exactly that?

5
00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:24.971
Two rules. Keep the index inside the array: n less than one

6
00:00:24.971 --> 00:00:28.722
thousand, so indices zero to nine hundred ninety nine.

7
00:00:28.722 --> 00:00:32.185
And test the sentinel right after each value is read.

8
00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:35.812
The correct loop reads A of n, checks it, reads B of

9
00:00:35.812 --> 00:00:38.768
n, checks it, and only then moves n forward.

10
00:00:39.388 --> 00:00:41.769
The other loops each break a rule.

11
00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:46.822
n less than or equal to one thousand allows A of one thousand, past the end.

12
00:00:46.822 --> 00:00:50.713
Testing A of n before reading it tests uninitialised memory.

13
00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:53.153
And testing after n plus plus looks at the

14
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.231
next element, which has not been read yet.

15
00:00:57.831 --> 00:01:02.051
The theory: a sentinel is a special input value that means stop.

16
00:01:02.051 --> 00:01:05.821
The check has to sit between reading the value and using it.

17
00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:09.816
And the counter n ends up equal to the number of complete pairs.

18
00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:14.611
So the correct loop is the while loop with n less than one

19
00:01:14.611 --> 00:01:18.027
thousand that checks each value right after it is read.

20
00:01:18.848 --> 00:01:23.306
Testing an element before reading it, or after the index has already moved on.

21
00:01:24.026 --> 00:01:27.604
Exam tip: for input loops, trace a short run with the sentinel

22
00:01:27.604 --> 00:01:30.875
in the middle, and check which index each test reads.
